PUBLIC RESPONSE: The Tonga flag consists of a red field, a white canton (top-left rectangle), and a red Greek cross inside the canton. Assigning matrix entries of value \(a\) to all red pixels and \(b\) to all white pixels therefore produces a matrix whose rows fall into at most four geometrically distinct types:

- rows lying entirely below the canton (all entries \(a\));
- rows inside the canton height but outside the cross (white on either side of the vertical arm, red everywhere to the right of the canton);
- rows that intersect only the vertical arm of the cross;
- rows that intersect the horizontal arm of the cross.

Each type yields a distinct row vector. Consequently the matrix has at most four distinct rows and its rank is at most 4.  

For sufficiently fine discretizations the four vectors are linearly independent, so rank exactly 4 is attained. Hence the maximal possible rank is 4.

(The same bound is obtained by writing the matrix as \(bJ+(a-b)R\) where \(R\) is the 0-1 indicator of red pixels; the geometry of the Tonga cross implies that \(R\) itself has rank at most 3, yielding the same overall rank bound of 4.)

