1.text:
The Tonga flag produces a matrix \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) whose white indicator \(W\) is the union of four rectangles (the canton minus the centered cross). This geometry yields precisely three distinct row patterns: the constant-\(a\) background, the canton rows whose support excludes the horizontal arm, and the canton rows whose support includes the horizontal arm. These three vectors are linearly independent whenever \(a\neq b\), so \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=3\) is attained.  

The repeated rank-2 claims rest on the false premise that \(W=uv^T\). The four white rectangles do not arise from any single pair of row- and column-block indicators; the column support of the white intervals changes between the two canton row types, so no rank-1 factorization of \(W\) exists. The same mismatch refutes every asserted linear dependence among the three observed row patterns. No discretization can produce a fourth independent row, confirming that the bound of 3 is tight.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3}

2.text:
The Tonga flag produces a white indicator \(W\) whose support inside the canton is exactly the Cartesian product of two fixed row intervals and two fixed column intervals. Consequently \(W=uv^T\) (rank exactly 1) at every resolution, the constant red background contributes the rank-1 all-ones term, and \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) therefore satisfies \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le2\) (attained whenever \(a\neq b\)).

The three purported row patterns are linearly dependent once arm overlap is taken into account: any row that intersects both arms equals the sum of the constant-\(a\) background row and the two single-arm rows (with coefficients fixed by the relative widths). This identity is forced by the rectangular geometry and holds at every discretization, so the row space never exceeds dimension 2.

